1. Calculate the length increase in fingernails over the entire 12 months:
   \[
   \text{Total length increase} = 12 \times (2 \div 10) = 12 \times 0.2 = 2.4 \text{ inches}
   \]

2. Add this total length increase to her fingernails' current length:
   \[
   \text{Final fingernail length} = 2 + 2.4 = 4.4 \text{ inches}
   \]

3. Determine Sandy's new fingernail length when her fingernails reach 26 inches: 
   \[
   \text{New fingernail length} = \begin{cases} 
   2 + 2.4 = 4.4 \text{ inches} & \text{if } \textit{sandy} \text{ is} \text{2 years old} \\
   2 \text{ inches} + 144 \text{ months} = 2 + \frac{144}{12} = 7.8 \text{ inches} & \text{if } \textit{sandy} \text{ turns} \text{over} \text{ ]2 years old}
   \end{cases}
   \]

Since Sandy will turn 2 years old when her fingernails grow to 26 inches (assuming it is over her current 2 years old), she will be:

\[
\boxed{\text{2+144/12 years} = \boxed{2\text{ years and }144/12 + \text{how many years previous, if any.}
